    // Ok-payload mismatch in LET position (the Ok value is consumed locally, not returned):
    // ACCEPTED — only the error type must match; the lowering rebuilds the Err return as the
    // enclosing fn's Result. The headline multi-type-pipeline shape.
    // The DEGENERATE return-direct shape with a mismatched Ok payload: `return f().or_return()`
    // unwraps bool then returns it bare from a Result<i32,E> fn — rejected by the post-lowering
    // return-fit scan (not or-return: the propagation itself is fine, the leftover value isn't).
    // M1 slice 1 (rt-scoped-runtime, docs/rt-scoped-runtime.md): spawner scratch must not cross an
    // actor boundary. Rejects (error[scratch-escape]): a send whose enum payload carries a local
    // `a.acquire(…)` pointer; a send of a slice-literal-backed struct (stack storage); spawn state
    // embedding a local acquire pointer (the judges' `Counter(seen: <spawner-local>)` repro), bound
    // OR embedded directly; a send embedding an arena bound via the Result-unified
    // `make_in(…).expect(…)` (the checker unwraps `.expect` over a scratch source).
    // Accepts (no over-rejection): plain-value sends, Arc-wrapped sends
    // (`new_in(…).expect(…)` — call results classify clean), Arc-backed spawn state, and the
    // plain-value `Room(n: 7)` spawn state.
    // or_return in a GLOBAL initializer: there is NO enclosing function to return the Err to —
    // the lowered early-return silently aborted zen__init_globals, leaving every later global
    // zero (H stayed 0, exit 0 instead of 7). Positioned or-return error at check time.
    // Ok-payload mismatch, CONDITIONAL position (&& rhs): the lowering returns the receiver
    // VERBATIM, so a Result<bool,E> receiver in a Result<i32,E> fn emitted `return Result_bool_E`
    // inside a value block the return-fit scan never reaches → cc error as an internal compiler
    // bug. The or-return check now requires the Ok payloads to agree too.
    // Ok-payload mismatch in a @while condition — the other conditional position that dodged
    // the return-fit scan and died at cc.
    // SHADOWING is not a skip: a call through a KNOWN non-FnT param that shadows an INTRINSIC
    // (`store_i64: i64`) is not-callable — genc dispatches calls by NAME, so accepting this
    // emitted the intrinsic expansion with the value arg degraded to literal 0 (a silent 0-write).
    // same hole, cast intrinsic: `to_i64: i64` param called with 3 args ran as the CAST of the
    // first arg (extras dropped). Known non-FnT shadow → not-callable, not a silent accept.
    // SHADOWING is not a skip: calling through an i64 param that shadows a top-level fn is
    // not-callable at CHECK time (used to pass check, then die at cc: "'f' is not a function").
    // SHADOWING is not a skip, IMPORTED flavor: an i64 param shadowing an imported fn (`at`)
    // is equally not-callable — same cc-error leak class as the top-level-fn shadow.
    // RECURSIVE HOF: a self-recursive fn with an FnT param is inline-only — its residual
    // self-call after the unroll bound references a C symbol that is never emitted (link error
    // reported as an internal compiler bug). Positioned reject at check time instead.
    // recursive-hof leniency: an FnT PARAM named after the fn itself SHADOWS it — the body's
    // `apply(n)` is an indirect call through the param, not recursion. Must stay accepted.
    // SHADOWING does not blunt the checks: a wrong-arity call THROUGH the shadowed FnT param
    // (`f: (i64) i64` called with two args) is still an arity error — via the indirect-call path,
    // not top-level `f`'s signature.
    // SHADOWING does not blunt M5 R5: a wrong-arity LAMBDA passed to a HOF whose FnT param is
    // named after a top-level fn is still rejected against the HOF's declared FnT.
    // UAF alias-class join: `q := p` names the SAME allocation, so freeing p then freeing (or
    // using) q is a double-free/use-after-free — the name-only kill-set used to miss it.
    // alias UAF read: load through the alias after the original was freed.
    // alias classes are TRANSITIVE: p -> q -> r, freeing p kills r too.
    // FREE-IN-LOOP: freeing a local declared OUTSIDE the loop body frees the same pointer
    // again on the next iteration — a double-free the single lexical walk used to miss.
    // a CONDITIONAL free of an outer local in a loop MAY double-free — flagged (may-analysis,
    // the same rule that makes a branch free conditionally dead after the branch).
    // load_i64/store_i64/atomic_* deref their pointer arg exactly like load/store: a nullable
    // `RawPtr<T>` (T != u8) must be null-checked first (this used to slip through).
    // no over-rejection: REVIVAL stays per-name — rebinding the alias detaches it, so using the
    // fresh q after p's free is fine.
    // no over-rejection: freeing a loop-LOCAL is fresh each iteration.
    // no over-rejection: an alias where NEITHER name is freed until the end is fine.
    // no over-rejection: the `p = a.resize(p, n)` grow idiom rebinds in the same statement, so
    // each iteration nets to alive.
    // no over-rejection: free-then-reacquire inside the loop body revives before the iteration ends.
    // no over-rejection: the error-path idiom (free + `return` in one arm, free after) is still
    // legal — the freeing arm does not fall through.
    // R4: a base digit out of range right after the prefix (`0o9`, `0b2`, `0b19`, `0xFFg`) used
    // to split into two tokens with the wrong value — now a positioned lexer error.
    // R5: a lone radix prefix with no digits (`0b`/`0o`/`0x`) used to be accepted as value 0.
    // Q4: an unterminated `/* */` block comment now carets its opening `/*` (was positionless).
    // a valid radix literal + a valid block comment still lex correctly (no over-rejection).
    // R2: a NESTED field write through a read-only `Ptr<T>` mutates `*b` — reject like the direct set.
    // R2 (accept): the SAME nested write through a `MutPtr<T>` is legal.
    // R3: an index-set into a slice field of a read-only `Ptr<T>` writes into `*b` — reject.
    // R3 (accept): the SAME index-set through a `MutPtr<T>` is legal.
    // accept: writing through a MutPtr field reached FROM a read-only Ptr base mutates that field's
    // target, not `*b` — the recursion stops at the first (writable) pointer indirection.
    // R6: exhaustiveness on a LOCAL-binding match subject (not a param) still rejects.
    // R7: a non-void body that falls through with no produced value is a return-fit error.
    // R1: passing a read-only `Ptr<T>` where a `MutPtr<T>` is wanted is an arg-type error.
    // R8: assert_nonnull on a non-pointer arg is an arg-type error.
    // M5 R3: a turbofish type arg that names no declared type → positioned unknown-type (was a cc leak).
    // M5 R4: turbofish on a callee with NO type params → no-type-args (was silently dropped).
    // M5 R4: an explicit type arg that contradicts the value arg (`T=str` vs `5:i64`) → arg-type.
    // M5 R5: a closure with the WRONG param count for the FnT param → positioned arity.
    // M5 R5: a closure whose body produces the wrong type (bool where T is wanted) → arg-type.
    // Q3: calling a DEFINED non-function local is `not-callable`, distinct from `undefined-name`.
    // or_return in a fn whose return type is NOT a Result → positioned or-return error.
    // or_return whose receiver's Err type differs from the enclosing fn's Err type → or-return.
    // or_return in EXPRESSION position (return position, mid-expression) in a matching-Result fn: accepted.
    // or_return with a DIFFERING Ok payload: accepted in consume positions (let/arg/mid-chain — only E
    // must match), still or-return in conditional positions (&& rhs, @while cond), and return-fit for
    // the degenerate bare `return f().or_return()`.
    // statement-position one-armed bool guard now ACCEPTS (was the position-less undefined-name sentinel).
    // value-position one-armed bool match now rejects for the RIGHT reason: a positioned exhaustiveness
    // error (NOT the old position-less undefined-name path).
    // infinite-type: a struct/enum containing ITSELF by value has infinite size (the cc-error leak fix).
    // INDIRECTION breaks the cycle — `Ptr<T>` / `[T]` are finite, so these are accepted.
    a + b
}
